11
00:55:00,000 --> 00:55:06,000
There's Something About Mary is a 1998 American romantic comedy film directed by the
Farrelly brothers, who co-wrote the screenplay with Ed Decter and John J.

12
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
The film features Cameron Diaz as the title character, while Ben Stiller, Matt Dillon, Lee Evans, and
Chris Elliott all play characters who are in love with Mary, and vying for her affection.

13
01:05:00,000 --> 01:05:06,000
There's Something About Mary was released theatrically
on July 15, 1998, by 20th Century Fox.

14
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
It received generally positive reviews from critics, who
praised its humor and Diaz's performance.

15
01:15:00,000 --> 01:15:06,000
The film became a major box office success, grossing over $369 million worldwide against its
$23 million budget, becoming the fourth-highest-grossing film of the year.

16
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
It is placed 27th in the American Film Institute's 100 Years, 100 Laughs: America's
Funniest Movies, a list of the 100 funniest movies of the 20th century.

17
01:25:00,000 --> 01:25:06,000
In 2000, readers of Total Film magazine voted There's Something
About Mary the fourth-greatest comedy film of all time.

18
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:06,000
Diaz won a New York Film Critics Circle Award for Best Actress, an MTV Movie Award for Best Performance, an
American Comedy Award for Best Actress, a Blockbuster Entertainment Award for Best Actress.

19
01:35:00,000 --> 01:35:06,000
Her performance additionally was nominated for the Golden Globe Award
for Best Actress – Motion Picture Musical or Comedy.

20
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:06,000
The film was also nominated for a Golden Globe Award for
Best Motion Picture – Musical or Comedy.

21
01:45:00,000 --> 01:45:06,000
It won four out of eight MTV Movie Awards, including Best Movie.

22
01:50:00,000 --> 01:50:06,000
The film explores themes of surgeon, romcom, stalker, dream girl, taboo.

23
01:55:00,000 --> 01:55:06,000
The tone is lighthearted and emotional and thought_provoking.
